          "label": "<p>Why is <em>Mycoplasma</em> species intrinsically resistant to all beta-lactam an</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why is <em>Mycoplasma</em> species intrinsically resistant to all beta-lactam antibiotics?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>They possess multiple efflux pumps that actively expel beta-lactams</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Efflux pumps are the intrinsic resistance mechanism of <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, not <em>Mycoplasma</em>. Pseudomonas combines efflux with reduced porins to resist most antibiotics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They completely lack a cell wall, eliminating the drug's target</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Beta-lactams act by binding penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs) in the cell wall. Since <em>Mycoplasma</em> has no cell wall, the drug has no target — making resistance inherent to the species.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>They have low-affinity penicillin-binding proteins that do not bind beta-lactams</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Low-affinity PBPs are the mechanism behind <em>Enterococcus faecalis</em> resistance to cephalosporins, not <em>Mycoplasma</em> resistance to beta-lactams.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Their outer membrane blocks entry of hydrophobic beta-lactam molecules</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Outer membrane exclusion is the mechanism by which Gram-negative bacteria resist Penicillin G. <em>Mycoplasma</em> lacks both a cell wall and an outer membrane.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Beta-lactams target penicillin-binding proteins in the bacterial cell wall. Consider what happens when an organism has no cell wall at all — the drug simply has no site of action."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Intrinsic resistance is a natural species-level property present before any antibiotic exposure. <em>Mycoplasma</em>'s complete absence of a cell wall removes the target for all beta-lactam antibiotics."
                }
              ],
