          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A resident continues IV meropenem for community-acquired pneumonia for 10 days, </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A resident continues IV meropenem for community-acquired pneumonia for 10 days, even though blood cultures at 48 hours show <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> susceptible to oral amoxicillin. Which antimicrobial stewardship failure does this BEST illustrate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Failure to obtain culture and susceptibility data before starting empirical therapy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cultures were in fact obtained — the results are available at 48 hours. The problem is not the absence of data but the failure to ACT on it by narrowing therapy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Failure to de-escalate to a narrow-spectrum agent based on susceptibility data</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Once culture and susceptibility results are available (typically by 48–72 hours), AMSP mandates de-escalation from broad-spectrum empirical therapy to the narrowest effective agent. Continuing meropenem when amoxicillin would suffice is a key driver of carbapenem resistance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Using an antibiotic that is ineffective against the identified pathogen</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Meropenem IS effective against <em>S. pneumoniae</em> — the issue is not efficacy but unnecessarily broad-spectrum coverage. Using a carbapenem when amoxicillin suffices accelerates resistance without clinical benefit.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Prescribing an antibiotic dose that exceeds therapeutic requirements</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dose optimisation ('right dose') is an AMSP principle, but this scenario illustrates a 'right drug' failure — the antibiotic class is unnecessarily broad, regardless of the dose used.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the AMSP principle of de-escalation: when culture results arrive (usually by 48–72 hours), therapy should be narrowed to the most targeted effective antibiotic. This preserves broad-spectrum agents for when they are truly needed."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "De-escalation from empirical broad-spectrum therapy to the narrowest effective agent once susceptibility data is available is a cornerstone AMSP strategy that directly reduces selection pressure for resistant organisms."
                }
              ],
